;; manifest/orgs-detectors.edn — the registry of detectors that CANNOT be fleet
;; gates, and therefore run on this machine instead.
;;
;; ## Why this file exists
;;
;; ADR-2608124800 measured that roughly twenty verification scripts in this
;; workspace cannot be murakumo fleet gates, for a structural reason CLAUDE.md
;; already records: **a fleet gate ships only the target repo's own tree**, so a
;; detector that reads `orgs/` finds an empty workspace on a node and can never
;; go green there. `root-permit-index` has been 0 pass / 282 fail for exactly
;; this reason. Two of the detectors say so in their own headers.
;;
;; So the answer for them is not a `gates.edn` line. It is a home on a machine
;; that actually has `orgs/`. `scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs` is that home and
;; this file is what it runs.
;;
;; ## What an entry must declare, and why each field is mandatory
;;
;;   :id            keyword, stable. Findings are keyed under it forever.
;;   :title         one line, shown to a human at session start.
;;   :why           what it would have caught, with a date. An entry that cannot
;;                  answer this is a detector nobody has justified running.
;;   :argv          the command. `{{repo}}` is substituted with the checkout
;;                  holding the tick script, `{{root}}` with the orgs-bearing
;;                  checkout. They differ when the tick runs from a worktree.
;;   :cwd           :root (the orgs-bearing checkout) or :repo.
;;   :findings      :protocol -- the detector prints FINDING<TAB>sev<TAB>key<TAB>detail
;;                  lines. This is the only supported mode, deliberately: a tick
;;                  that regex-scraped prose reports would mis-key findings the
;;                  first time a message was reworded, and silently report every
;;                  standing finding as new.
;;   :evidence      regex that MUST appear in the output for the run to be
;;                  recorded :ok. This is the anti-theatre floor. ADR-2608124800
;;                  found five scripts that print PASSED without measuring; a
;;                  detector that dies after two lines must be recorded
;;                  :inconclusive, never clean. `SCANNED\t0\t...` fails it too.
;;   :interval-ms   minimum age before re-running. Per detector, because the
;;                  measured runtimes differ by 5x.
;;   :timeout-ms    hard kill. No entry may omit it.
;;   :writes        MUST be :none. The tick refuses to run an entry that does
;;                  not declare it. See the invariants below.
;;   :measured      what was actually observed when the entry was added, so the
;;                  interval and timeout are traceable to a measurement rather
;;                  than to a guess.
;;   :accepted      OPTIONAL. finding-key -> {:since :why :clears-when}, all
;;                  three non-empty strings, enforced by admission. A finding
;;                  listed here is CORRECT and deliberately not being fixed yet;
;;                  the tick reports it as ACCEPTED, never as NEW, and never
;;                  inside the STANDING count.
;;
;;                  It exists because the 2026-08-13 triage (ADR-2608132600)
;;                  needed a state the three classes could not express. Without
;;                  it, "we looked at this, we understand it, and the fix is a
;;                  decision someone else has to make" decays into STANDING --
;;                  the class this whole design calls indistinguishable from
;;                  silence. The decision would then be recorded nowhere, and a
;;                  month later would read exactly like a defect nobody opened.
;;
;;                  Two things keep it from becoming a way to make reds go away.
;;                  (1) Admission rejects an entry with no date, no reason or no
;;                  exit condition, so accepting costs as much writing as fixing
;;                  a small thing. (2) An acceptance whose finding has gone away
;;                  is reported LOUDLY as a stale acceptance, in the tick log and
;;                  at session start -- an exemption that outlives its subject
;;                  exempts whatever lands on that key next.
;;
;; ## Invariants this home holds itself to
;;
;;   1. It reads `orgs/` checkouts. It NEVER writes to one, never fetches, never
;;      checks out, never moves HEAD anywhere. Detectors are admitted on
;;      `:writes :none`, reviewed by a human before being added here, and are
;;      run with `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=none` so any `git fetch`/`clone`/`push`
;;      inside a detector fails at the transport layer rather than succeeding
;;      quietly. See the tick's header for what that env var does and does not
;;      cover, stated honestly.
;;   2. It writes NOTHING into any git checkout, including the superproject.
;;      State lives under `~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/`. A tick that dirtied the
;;      shared checkout would trip the stash-churn failure CLAUDE.md documents.
;;   3. It is a monitor, not a gate: exit 0 even with findings. The gate is the
;;      individual detector, run by a human or by a hook.
;;
;; ## What this home cannot host, stated rather than implied
;;
;;   - `annex-custody-verify` needs B2 credentials. It can run here (the keys
;;     are on this machine, not on a fleet node) but NOT under launchd: launchd
;;     cannot answer a Keychain unlock prompt, the wall recorded in
;;     ADR-2607178000. It belongs in the hand-run set, and putting it here on a
;;     schedule would produce a permanent :inconclusive -- another standing red.
;;   - Anything needing `gh` is rate-limited and shares one token with every
;;     concurrent agent session on this machine. Not scheduled here.
;;   - `audit-gftd-scripts` reads `$HOME/.gftd`, which is correct here and
;;     meaningless on a node -- but it is cheap and ADR-2608124800 ranks it as a
;;     SessionStart hook, which is a better fit than a 6-hour tick.
;;
;; ## The 2026-08-13 sweep of `scripts/verify-*.cljs`, and what it ruled out
;;
;; Thirteen scripts under `scripts/verify-*.cljs` mention `orgs/`. Four are
;; registered below. The other nine, with the class each falls in:
;;
;;   - `verify-duplicate-registrations` -- **`gh` class.** It calls
;;     `gh api repos/<slug>` once per candidate group to confirm two paths are
;;     one upstream by GitHub id. Rate-limited, one token shared with every
;;     concurrent agent session.
;;   - `verify-namespace-collisions` -- **not swept.** A sibling session was
;;     working on it on 2026-08-13; registering it concurrently would have
;;     pinned an argv that was being changed underneath.
;;   - `verify-repository-roles` -- **already hosted.** It is the fleet gate
;;     `root-repository-roles`, diagnosed and returned to green by
;;     ADR-2608124800. It needs no second home.
;;   - `verify-parity-probes` -- **already hosted, and it does not read
;;     `orgs/`.** It is the fleet gate `root-parity-probe-policy`; the string
;;     `orgs/` appears in it only inside a regex that matches the TEXT of a
;;     probe, and in the header prose. Its only input is
;;     `90-docs/maturity/engine-parity.datoms.edn`, which is in root's own tree.
;;   - `verify-no-babashka` -- **does not read `orgs/` either**, for the same
;;     reason: `--root orgs` appears in its usage comment, and its default root
;;     is `.`. ADR-2608124800 already classes it root-tree-only and gatable
;;     as-is, so what it is missing is a `gates.edn` line, not a registry entry.
;;     Measured here anyway, because pointing it at `orgs/` was the obvious
;;     thing to try: `--root orgs --allow-zero` ran for roughly 38 minutes on
;;     this machine without producing a line and was killed. It reads every file
;;     in the tree **in full** to test for a `bb` shebang, so its cost is
;;     dominated by the size of the artefacts it is not looking for.
;;   - `verify-clj-everywhere` -- **not a detector.** It is a test-suite
;;     orchestrator: four audits and then `clojure -M:test` in five repos. It
;;     emits no per-finding output to key on, it resolves Maven dependencies
;;     over HTTPS, and it writes to `~/.m2`, so `:writes :none` would be false.
;;   - `verify-kotoba-pages` -- **needs a real browser.** It drives Playwright
;;     against `file://` pages. CLAUDE.md's resource governor caps concurrent
;;     browser probes, and this machine's many parallel sessions already
;;     contend for OS focus; a browser launched unattended on a schedule is a
;;     materially different thing from a file-reading monitor.
;;   - `verify-vendored-copies`, `verify-require-graph` -- registered below
;;     since 2026-08-12.

{:schema 1

 :detectors
 [{:id       :verify-vendored-copies
   :title    "vendored copies vs the upstream they name"
   :why      "ADR-2608121000, 2026-08-12: six repos vendor pay/* and treasury/core.cljc
              into their own src/ because their CI checks out one repo with no west
              workspace. Each copy's header says `diff against upstream to check drift`
              and nothing performed that diff. Several had drifted for weeks on paths
              that decide whether a payment happened -- one was missing the guard whose
              own fix comment says its absence lets an unconfirmed payment be accepted
              as confirmed. The instruction to check was addressed to a person."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-vendored-copies.cljs" "--check" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  900000                   ; 15m -- 8x the measured runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-12 on this machine: 1m41s wall, 34 vendored files, exit 1 with
              --check, 13 stale (7 of them on pay/x402 and treasury/core -- the payment
              paths). Only `git show`/`git log`/`git rev-list` against already
              checked-out repos; no network."}

  {:id       :verify-require-graph
   :title    "namespaces migrated to .kotoba with the callers left behind"
   :why      "ADR-2608071000, 2026-07-21: kotoba-lang/dsl-core deleted
              src/kotoba/dsl/problem.cljc for problem.kotoba and updated no consumer.
              kotoba.dsl.problem stopped loading on JVM, nbb and ClojureScript, and
              12 checked-out repos stayed broken for 17 days with nobody noticing --
              src/ execution paths, not just tests. Nobody noticed because every CI
              looked only at its own repo: dsl-core's own tests were green."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-require-graph.cljs" "--root" "{{root}}" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- see :measured; this one is expensive
   :timeout-ms  2700000                  ; 45m -- 5x the measured runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-12 on this machine: 8m0s under the tick (8m48s standalone; 29% CPU --
              it is dominated by per-repo subprocesses, and this machine runs many
              concurrent agents), 3571 repos with a source root, exit 1: 2 orphan
              namespaces that gate, 97 report-only sibling gaps that do not.
              24h rather than 6h because a .kotoba migration lands on human timescales
              and four idle 9-minute sweeps a day is a real cost on a shared machine."}

  {:id       :verify-layer-deps
   :title    "deps.edn :local/root edges against the layer DAG in manifest/layers.edn"
   :why      "manifest/layers.edn declares a dependency DAG over the workspace's layers and
              this is the only thing that checks it. ADR-2607289600 records the state it is
              guarding against, measured: the layer plane is `定義のみ` -- west.yml has zero
              `layer-*` groups and gen-west-manifest.cljs does not read layers.edn -- so a
              violating :local/root edge is caught by NOTHING else in the pipeline. It has
              also already found real breakage: the Phase-1 dedup worklist
              (90-docs/2607171100-phase1-dedup-worklist.csv, row 210) records
              cloud-itonami-isic-855 with a :local/root one directory too high, credited to
              this script.
              ⚠ 2026-08-13 correction (ADR-2608132600): this line previously said the seven
              standing violations were `numbered directories re-created inside three app repos
              after layers.edn froze the list`. That was wrong, and it is the failure mode this
              registry's `:why` field is supposed to prevent -- a plausible story written from
              the finding text rather than from the repos. All seven directories were added
              between 2026-04-08 and 2026-06-26, BEFORE layers.edn froze the list on 2026-07-17
              (`git log --diff-filter=A`). They reappeared because layers.edn keys its exemption
              lists on WEST PATH, and three GitHub org-transfers and one rename moved the paths
              while the exemptions stayed: gftdcojp/app-aozora -> network-awai/app-aozora
              (gh id 1275051908), gftdcojp/club-shinshi -> network-awai/club-shinshi (1281275923),
              gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp -> gftdcojp/apps-gftdcojp (1136866292). Fixed in
              layers.edn; 7 -> 0. What this detector actually guards against is therefore two
              things, and only the first was written down: a violating :local/root edge, and an
              exemption list whose keys have drifted off the repos they exempt."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-layer-deps.cljs" "--report" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  900000                   ; 15m -- 7x the worst measured runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-13 on this machine, twice, and the spread is the point. At load ~1
              (10 cores): 26.5s wall. At load 133: 2m06s wall for the same work, 10.1s of it
              user CPU -- it is waiting, not computing. Both runs: 4,415 repo directories
              under orgs/, 3,489 deps.edn parsed, 2,978 :local/root edges, 7 violations, all
              :internal-numbered-dir. exit 0 with --report (exit 1 without it). Reads only
              readdirSync/readFileSync plus one `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`; no network,
              no writes. The 15m timeout is sized against the CONTENDED number, because this
              machine's load has been measured at 187 while this entry was being written."}

  {:id       :verify-kotoba-host-parity
   :title    "the declared host-import set vs the two implementations that must match it"
   :why      "The catalogue in kotoba-lang/lang/host-parity.edn, kototama's contract.cljc and
              wasm-webcomponent's actor-host.js are three copies of one fact -- which host
              imports a .kotoba guest may call -- in three repos, and only this script
              compares them. It is red right now with TWO violations, and the second one was
              invisible until 2026-08-13: every check called process.exit(1) on the spot, so
              a run only ever showed the first. That is the fail-fast blindness
              ADR-2608124800 measured on root-repository-roles, where every later assertion
              had gone unexecuted since 2026-08-03 with nobody able to see it. The checks are
              independent, so they now all run."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-kotoba-host-parity.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  300000                   ; 5m
   :writes   :none
   ;; Both findings are CORRECT and are accepted, not fixed. ADR-2608132600.
   ;;
   ;; Authority was established first, and not by reading the file's own claim to
   ;; be an SSoT. `kotoba-lang/src/kotoba/lang/grammar_authority.clj` declares
   ;; `lang/host-parity.edn` the authority and names its copies
   ;; (`resources/kotoba/lang/host-parity.edn`,
   ;; `../kotoba/resources/kotoba/lang/host-parity.edn`) as vendored mirrors --
   ;; an independent mechanism in a third file, written for a different reason.
   ;; So: the catalogue is the authority for what is ALLOWED; the two
   ;; implementations are ground truth for what EXISTS; where they disagree the
   ;; catalogue is what moves. Measured while doing this: the `../kotoba` mirror
   ;; has already drifted (sha 0cfe8556 vs 69d66b3b), and grammar_authority's own
   ;; docstring says host-parity in kotoba was surveyed but never checked.
   ;;
   ;; Why accepted rather than fixed. Correcting the catalogue is a three-repo
   ;; edit to a capability surface that re-baselines counts the file itself
   ;; guards (`browser-linkable-count-changed`, `:honest-gaps
   ;; :global-browser-parity-10-of-59`), retires a conformance case, and has to
   ;; carry a drifted fourth copy along with it. Landing that blind is how a
   ;; half-fix gets recorded as a fix. The residual is small and exactly named
   ;; below, which is the deliverable.
   :accepted
   {"import-sets-drifted"
    {:since "2026-08-13"
     :why "Real 3-way drift, understood and enumerated. 9 imports exist in kototama's
           contract.cljc that the catalogue does not declare (:http-fetch :cbor-encode
           :json-encode :json-extract-field :http-post-headers, all five also in the
           browser host since 2026-07-25 commit 498e149; plus :http-get-stream
           :object-get-stream :object-put-block :object-compare-and-set-ref, JVM-only).
           10 imports are declared with :component-link status and are not actor:host
           :import/id entries at all (:http-open/write/read/close :http-get :db-open/
           write/read/close/exchange). The dangerous half is the first: the catalogue's
           :unlisted-import-default is {:browser :no}, so a guest author reading the
           authority believes cbor-encode is browser-absent when the browser host
           implements it unconditionally. That is disclosure owed, not a capability to
           be widened, but writing it changes :required-imports 59 -> 68 and the
           :honest-gaps baseline."
     :clears-when "kotoba-lang PR declaring the 9 and reclassifying the 10, with both
                   vendored mirrors re-synced (resources/ and ../kotoba/resources/, the
                   latter already drifted), west pin advanced, and this verifier's
                   `(= 10 (count browser-linkable))` qualification updated in the same
                   change."}
    "matrix-vs-implementation"
    {:since "2026-08-13"
     :why "Real, and reduced from 17 ids to 6 on 2026-08-13 by fixing this verifier's own
           stale model -- 11 of the 17 were pg wire/pool functions added to actor-host.js
           on 2026-07-31 that the verifier's hand-written not-browser-callable set never
           learned about. The 6 that remain are the hosts genuinely disagreeing:
           :matrix-only #{:http-get} is the catalogue claiming browser :inject, and
           carrying a conformance case :http-get-browser-inject-available expecting
           :available, for an import actor-host.js does not implement at all -- it is not
           even in IMPORT_SURFACE. :implementation-only is the same 5 undeclared imports
           as above. An overclaimed browser capability is the exact lie the catalogue's
           own note forbids (`guests must not assume an import is ambient just because it
           compiles`), which is why this is accepted with a named exit and not left
           unwritten."
     :clears-when "Same kotoba-lang PR: :http-get browser :inject -> :no (or the browser
                   host implements http_get), the conformance case retired or inverted,
                   and the 5 codec/http imports declared."}}
   :measured "2026-08-13 on this machine at load ~133: 11.7s wall, 0.77s of it user CPU --
              nbb start-up and scheduling, not work; it reads three files. 59 declared host
              imports across those three files, exit 1, 2 findings (import-sets-drifted,
              matrix-vs-implementation). Three readFileSync calls; no subprocess, no network,
              no writes. The 5m timeout is 25x the measured wall time and is sized for
              start-up under contention rather than for the work."}

  {:id       :verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover
   :title    "JVM/Chicory dependencies that appear outside the frozen legacy inventory"
   :why      "ADR-2607198300 makes 'the shipped artefact does not depend on a JVM, Node or
              Rust host' a repo-wide rule, and 90-docs/migration/kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover.edn
              is the frozen list of the exceptions that are allowed to remain. Nothing else
              compares that list against the four deps.edn files it freezes, and those files
              live under orgs/, so no fleet gate can. It is red today: amu/deps.edn carries a
              JVM/runtime dependency that the inventory does not cover -- a new one would
              land the same way, silently, because the contract is prose until something
              reads it."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  300000                   ; 5m
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-13 on this machine at load ~133: 6.0s wall, 0.82s user CPU. 10
              contract/dependency paths resolved, exit 1, 1 finding
              (jvm-dep-not-frozen:orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/deps.edn). readFileSync/existsSync
              only; no subprocess, no network, no writes. Same timeout reasoning as
              host-parity: the 5m is for start-up under contention, not for the work."}

  {:id       :verify-tsukuru-factory-plane
   :title    "tsukuru's three factory datasets staying partitioned by CONSENT, not by realism"
   :why      "The three datasets differ by what each company agreed to -- research references
              with no consent, example seeds, and R0 worked examples -- and every way that
              distinction breaks is silent: a tag collapses, rows drop, a did:web leaks into
              the unconsented set, and Datalog keeps answering. The largest possible lie here
              is an un-onboarded company appearing as registered. It is also the detector
              that most recently WAS the failure it guards against: until 2026-08-13 an empty
              plane query made it skip all three checks and exit 0, the same shape as the five
              retired security-gate-* scripts, inside the verifier itself (ADR-2608130600).
              Its only scheduled caller is scripts/tsukuru-maturity-tick.cljs, driven by
              com.gftd.tsukuru-maturity-tick -- one of the five plists ADR-2608124800 found
              was never installed. Registering it here gives it a home WITHOUT installing
              that plist, which also launches an autonomous model loop that commits and
              pushes; the ADR is explicit that those are different decisions."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-tsukuru-factory-plane.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- see :measured
   :timeout-ms  5400000                  ; 90m -- 4.9x the worst measured runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-13 on this machine, twice. Standalone at load 157-187 on 10 cores:
              18m21s wall, of which 1093.4s is the single plane load + 3 queries, 256s user
              CPU. Through the tick itself at load ~110-157: 822s (13m42s), exit 0,
              scanned=2221, 0 findings -- so the timeout is 6.6x the tick measurement and
              4.9x the worst standalone one. exit 0, 0 findings,
              SCANNED 2,221 factory entities (candidates 2,119 / registry-seed 99 / seed 3,
              each above its floor; 3 datasets disjoint; 0 did:web in candidates; 0
              fabricated :company/lei). The script's own header records ~295s on a quiet
              host, so the load term dominates by 3.7x -- that spread is why it says to
              re-measure on the host before scheduling it, and why the timeout is sized on
              the LOADED number. 24h rather than 6h for the same reason
              verify-require-graph is: 256s of CPU four times a day on a machine already at
              load 157 is a real cost, and a consent-tier partition changes on human
              timescales. It reads EDN files and spawns exactly one child,
              `nbb manifest/edn-query.cljs q*`, which has no write call anywhere; no
              network, no writes."}

  {:id       :verify-namespace-collisions
   :title    "namespaces two repositories both ship"
   :why      "ADR-2608131700, 2026-08-13: two repos shipping one namespace means a build
              silently gets whichever the classpath reaches first, and the loser's code
              is dead while still looking live. Measured on this machine that day:
              `authentication.adapters.external-factors` does not compile at all on
              cloud-itonami/local-itonami's real classpath -- `No such var:
              oidc/verify-once` -- because org-openid-oidc's `oidc.core` sits ahead of
              kotoba-lang/oidc's and the two share no names. Nothing reported it. The
              same run found `kotoba.lang.package-registry` shipped by two repos with
              ten shared names and divergent bodies, one of which drops the
              :dep/definition-cids that stops a republish swapping a definition body.
              It cannot be a fleet gate: a gate is shipped only the target repo's own
              tree, and this question is about two trees at once."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-namespace-collisions.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- as expensive as verify-require-graph
   :timeout-ms  3600000                  ; 60m -- 2.6x the slowest measured run
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-13 on this machine, twice: 22.8m and 14.1m wall for the same corpus
              (the spread is other agents on this machine, not the corpus), 4045
              checked-out repos / 5438 source trees / 16406 file paths. 9 fail findings
              (co-classpath divergent) and 10 warn (EXTENSION-SHADOWED with no closure
              holding both). No --all: the 253 divergent-but-unreachable and 1544
              identical namespaces are scaffold families and would be 1500 standing
              lines. --findings was proved additive on a 3-repo fixture: output
              byte-identical without the flag, and identical with it once the FINDING
              and SCANNED lines are removed; --check still exits 1 either way."}]}

